 **Light and dark.** Every frame takes the colour scheme of the page around it: the view reads its container's `color-scheme` (and the reader's preference, where it allows both) when it opens, at each render, when the preference changes and when printing starts and ends, and sends it to its frames, which set it on their documents. So frames stay transparent on a dark page, their text in its colours, and every component, framed or inline, gets it as `context.scheme` (spec §7.4), with an update when it changes. A page that switches its scheme itself sets `color-scheme` on the container, or above it, and calls `view.scheme()` (or renders). In host mode, a view with no frame and no component reads nothing: reading an element's style makes the browser style the whole host page first.
 
-**Many views in one page.** In host mode a host may open a view for each of its texts (a diff viewer's runs, a list of comments): the views of a page share one stylesheet and its base rules, and a view of text and code changes nothing in the page's styles when it opens, renders or is destroyed ([many pages in one document](https://github.com/comp-markdown/compmd/blob/main/npm-packages/render/README.md#the-three-layers)). Each view still has its own settling and its own listeners.
+**Many views in one page.** In host mode a host may open a view for each of its texts (a diff viewer's runs, a list of comments): the views of a page share one stylesheet and its base rules, and a view of text and code changes nothing in the page's styles when it opens, renders or is destroyed ([many pages in one document](https://github.com/comp-markdown/compmd/blob/main/npm-packages/render/README.md#the-three-layers)). Each view settles on its own, waiting for what it drew; for the page's stylesheets and fonts it looks at the page's links and its fonts, never through the whole page.
 
